# jpaccutil - Japanese Accent Processing Utility
# Cline Rules for Development Guidelines

## Project Overview
This is a Python package for Japanese accent processing that implements Sagisaka rules.
The package provides utilities for automatic accent mark insertion in Japanese text.

## Core Functionality
- Main logic is in jpaccutil/core.py (JapaneseAccentProcessor class)
- Utility functions in jpaccutil/utils.py
- CLI interface in jpaccutil/cli.py
- Uses jamorasep library for mora splitting

## Development Guidelines

### Code Style
- Follow PEP 8 Python style guidelines
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Include comprehensive docstrings with examples
- Use black for code formatting
- Use flake8 for linting
- Use mypy for type checking

### Testing
- All new features must include unit tests in tests/ directory
- Use pytest for testing framework
- Maintain test coverage above 80%
- Test files should follow naming convention: test_*.py

### Dependencies
- Core dependency: jamorasep (for mora splitting)
- Development dependencies in requirements-dev.txt
- Keep dependencies minimal and well-justified

### File Structure Rules
- Keep core logic in jpaccutil/core.py
- Utility functions go in jpaccutil/utils.py
- CLI interface in jpaccutil/cli.py
- Tests in tests/ directory with corresponding test_*.py files
- Documentation examples should be runnable

### Japanese Text Processing
- Always validate Japanese text input using validate_japanese_text()
- Handle hiragana, katakana, and kanji appropriately
- Use jamorasep for accurate mora splitting
- Preserve original text structure when adding accent marks

### Accent Mark Conventions
- Use '[' for rising tone (rise)
- Use ']' for falling tone (fall)
- Use '|' for accent phrase boundary
- Maintain consistency in accent notation throughout

### Error Handling
- Gracefully handle missing jamorasep dependency
- Validate input parameters before processing
- Provide meaningful error messages for invalid input
- Handle edge cases (empty strings, non-Japanese text)

### Documentation
- Update README.md for any new features
- Include usage examples in docstrings
- Maintain changelog for version updates
- Document any new accent notation formats

### Version Control
- Use semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH)
- Update version in setup.py and __init__.py
- Tag releases appropriately
- Write clear commit messages

### Performance Considerations
- Optimize for common use cases (short to medium Japanese text)
- Cache accent patterns when appropriate
- Minimize memory usage for large text processing
- Profile performance-critical functions

### Compatibility
- Support Python 3.7+
- Ensure cross-platform compatibility
- Handle different Japanese text encodings properly
- Test on multiple Python versions

## Specific Implementation Notes

### Sagisaka Rules Implementation
- Implement actual Sagisaka rules in apply_sagisaka_rules()
- Current implementation is placeholder - needs linguistic research
- Consider word boundaries and part-of-speech information
- Handle compound words appropriately

### CLI Interface
- Provide batch processing capabilities
- Support multiple input/output formats
- Include verbose and quiet modes
- Handle file encoding properly

### API Design
- Keep public API simple and intuitive
- Provide both high-level and low-level interfaces
- Maintain backward compatibility
- Use clear parameter names

## Before Making Changes
1. Read existing code to understand current patterns
2. Check if similar functionality already exists
3. Consider impact on existing API
4. Write tests before implementing features
5. Update documentation as needed

## Common Patterns
- Use List[str] for mora sequences
- Use Tuple[int, str] for accent positions
- Validate input with validate_japanese_text()
- Handle ImportError for optional dependencies
- Include usage examples in docstrings

## Domain Knowledge
- 匂坂 reads Sagisaka
